.TH "qscand" "1" "26 Feb 2014"
.SH "NAME"
qscand \- network wrapper for qscan 
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
\fBqscand [\-h] 
.br 
\fBqscand [\-n] [\-d] 
.br 
\fBqscand \-i NW-INTERFACE-ADDRESS \-p LISTEN-PORT \fR
.br 
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
qscand is a network wrapper which gives you access to your devices through the TCP/IP stacK. 

.SH "OPTIONS"

\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
.br
		show help
 
\fB\-n, \-\-nodaemon\fR	
.br
		don't daemonize qscand  

\fB\-d, \-\-debug\fR	
.br
		run qscand in debug mode  

\fB\-i, \-\-iface\fR
.br
		select ip address for listening  

\fB\-p, \-\-port\fR	
.br
		select port for listening  

.TP
.B \-\-allow-device PATH
Allow only the exact device paths listed by the administrator. Repeat this
option for multiple devices; the list replaces the default policy. Paths must
be 1 to 127 bytes long and contain no line endings. No wildcard or prefix
matching is performed. Administrator-listed aliases are trusted as supplied.
.TP
.B \-\-allow-all-devices
Disable device-path restrictions and restore legacy unrestricted selection.
This is strongly discouraged: network clients may select arbitrary paths using
the daemon's privileges. A warning is printed at startup. This option cannot
be combined with \fB\-\-allow-device\fR.
.SH "DEVICE ACCESS"
By default, Linux accepts only /dev/sr[0-9]+ and /dev/scd[0-9]+ device names.
The /dev/cdrom and /dev/dvd aliases are accepted only if their resolved targets
also match those optical device names. FreeBSD accepts /dev/cd[0-9]+ and
/dev/acd[0-9]+. On Unix the resolved target must exist and be a block or
character device; qscan receives that resolved path. These patterns are whole
path matches, not shell wildcards.
.PP
Windows accepts drive letters such as D: only when Windows reports an optical
drive. Other platforms, including macOS, require an explicit device list:
/dev/diskN and /dev/rdiskN can identify non-optical disks and are not default
allowlist entries. Linux /dev/sgN and legacy /dev/hdX paths also require an
explicit list.
.PP
A denied device selection clears the current selection. Device access is
checked again before starting a device-specific command. The list/scanbus
command still discovers drives and is not filtered by this policy.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
Use the platform defaults:
.PP
.nf
qscand -n
.fi
.PP
Expose only two administrator-selected drives:
.PP
.nf
qscand -n --allow-device /dev/sr0 --allow-device /dev/sg3
.fi
.SH "SECURITY"
Device-path restrictions do not authenticate clients or restrict optical-drive
operations such as write tests. The daemon listens on all interfaces by default;
use a trusted network or select a suitable interface with \fB\-i\fR.
The device namespace and administrator-listed aliases must be controlled by
trusted administrators. Local qscan usage is unaffected by this daemon policy.

